Q: additional options in Chrome headless print-to-pdf A: <p>Do the following:</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>&quot;/path/to/google-chrome&quot; --headless --run-all-compositor-stages-before-draw --virtual-time-budget=$x --print-to-pdf=$PATH $URL
</code></pre>
<p><code>&quot;/path/to/google-chrome&quot;</code> : This is the path of Google Chrome.</p>
<p><code>--headless</code>: Chrome browser in a headless environment without the full browser UI</p>
<p><code>--run-all-compositor-stages-before-draw</code> : It Prevents the Pdf Creation Before all the data is rendered(After all data is rendered the pdf will be created).</p>
<p><code>--virtual-time-budget=$x</code>: <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/53690355/6879778">delays the process of creating a PDF file</a>, where <code>$x</code> is in miliseconds.</p>
<p><code>--print-to-pdf=$PATH</code>: specifies to create a PDF file at the given path.</p>
<p><code>$URL</code>: the webpage URL.</p>
<hr />
<p>To adjust the print size on your own HTML:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@media print {
    @page {
        margin: 0mm 0mm 0mm 0mm;
        size: 8in 9.5in;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>The above CSS code does not affect the rendering of the webpage, but there is an effect on the formatting of the page only in the PDF.</p>

Q: I am trying to build CS50 PSET 1 problem: CASH with a for loop, but I am not getting any result A: <p>To provide closure to this issue based upon the narrative above along with the good comments, following is a refactored version of the code that addresses the inadvertent usage of &quot;return&quot; statements as well as determining the coin counts via function calls.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &quot;cs50.h&quot;

int calculate_coins(int cents, int value)   /* Generic function to determine number of coins by coin value  */
{
    int work = 0;

    while (cents &gt;= value)
    {
        work++;
        cents -= value;
    }

    return work;
}

int main(void)
{
    int cents, coins = 0, work;

    do
    {
        cents = get_int(&quot;Cash owed (in cents): &quot;);
    }
    while (cents &lt; 0);

    work = calculate_coins(cents, 25);      /* Quarters     */

    if (work &gt; 0)
        printf(&quot;Quarters: %d  &quot;, work);

    coins += work;
    cents -= (25 * work);

    work = calculate_coins(cents, 10);      /* Dimes        */

    if (work &gt; 0)
        printf(&quot;Dimes: %d  &quot;, work);

    coins += work;
    cents -= (10 * work);

    work = calculate_coins(cents, 5);       /* Nickels      */

    if (work &gt; 0)
        printf(&quot;Nickels: %d  &quot;, work);

    coins += work;
    cents -= (5 * work);

    work = calculate_coins(cents, 1);       /* Pennies      */

    if (work &gt; 0)
        printf(&quot;Pennies: %d  &quot;, work);

    coins += work;

    printf(&quot;\n&quot;);

    printf(&quot;Total coins.: %d\n&quot;, coins);

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Following are some of the highlights.</p>
<ul>
<li><p>The spurious return statements have been removed to avoid the &quot;dead code&quot; as noted in the comments.</p>
</li>
<li><p>In lieu of multiple functions to determine coin counts for specific coins, a general purpose coin derivation function is used passing the coin value to be evaluated, as the separate functions would have been basically the same lines of code with just the coin value being different in each function.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>The takeaway from this issue would be to delve more into some C tutorials as they pertain to the usage of the return statement and also the usage of functions.</p>

Q: Making an Powershell Progress Bar more efficient A: <p>To elaborate on my earlier comment:</p>
<p><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.utility/write-progress?view=powershell-7.1" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Write-Progress</code></a> is known to be slow, especially on <em>Windows</em> PowerShell. In other words, if you use Windows PowerShell, I recommend you upgrade (or at least check) <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/scripting/install/install-powershell-on-windows?view=powershell-7.5&amp;viewFallbackFrom=powershell-7.1" rel="nofollow noreferrer">PowerShell 7</a>+.</p>
<p>To speed thing up, you might consider invoking <code>Write-Progress</code> only about as much times as your maximum screen width:</p>
<pre><code>$Length = 400.000
(Measure-Command {
    $MaxScreenWidth = 200
    ForEach($i in (0..$Length)) {
        if ($i % [math]::floor($length / $MaxScreenWidth) -eq 0) { 
            Write-Progress -activity &quot;Searching for matches&quot; -status &quot;Scanned: $i of $($length)&quot; -percentComplete (($i / $length)  * 100)
        }
    }
}).TotalMilliSeconds
</code></pre>
<p>For this simple example, this is about twice as fast (on Windows PowerShell) as without the <code>if ($i % [math]::floor($length / $MaxScreenWidth) -eq 0) {</code> condition and even faster for more iterations or when you also output to the display at the same time.</p>
<p><strong>Notes:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The above example assumes a consecutive sequence</li>
<li>The sequense is assumed to be larger then the maximal screen width</li>
<li>The screen width shouldn't be changed(reduced) during the progress</li>
</ul>
<p>To avoid the above disadvantages, you might go for a more sophisticated implementation, such as:</p>
<pre><code>$Length = 400.000
(Measure-Command {
    ForEach($i in (0..$Length)) {
        $Script:WindowWidthChanged = $Script:WindowWidth -ne $Host.UI.RawUI.WindowSize.Width
        if ($Script:WindowWidthChanged) { $Script:WindowWidth = $Host.UI.RawUI.WindowSize.Width }
        $ProgressCompleted = [math]::floor($i * $Script:WindowWidth / $length)
        if ($Script:WindowWidthChanged -or $ProgressCompleted -ne $Script:LastProgressCompleted) {
            Write-Progress -activity &quot;Searching for matches&quot; -status &quot;Scanned: $i of $($length)&quot; -percentComplete (($i / $length)  * 100)
        }
        $Script:LastProgressCompleted = $ProgressCompleted
    }
}).TotalMilliSeconds
</code></pre>
